#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105367 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0ee39554-fd77-5e90-baa8-f9320ffc3398" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Continuous Random Variables Given in Context"}] :question/id #uuid "e7c527c9-2dc1-50b2-bbf6-10c1a9d122dd" :question/math-academy-id "e-10837" :question/problem "Which of the following are continuous random variables?\n\n- The number of tickets sold for a randomly selected baseball game in a certain stadium\n\n- The length of a randomly selected baseball field.\n\n- The weight, in grams, of a slice cut from a cake weighing $1\\,\\text{kg}$" :question/worked-solution "A random variable is a variable whose value is determined by the outcome of a trial or experiment. A *continuous* random variable is a random variable whose possible values are represented by an interval of real numbers.\n\nCommon examples of continuous random variables are shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040017/assets/05e3d893f6c069dce5b51dc7d9fa5830b44bca58fa071009548fde20ebe92c19.png)\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each of the given statements.\n\n- Statement I does *not* describe a continuous random variable. Its value is determined by the result of an experiment, but its possible values are the integers $0,1,2,3,\\ldots,$ which do not correspond to an interval of real numbers.\n\n- Statement II describes a continuous random variable. Its value is determined by the result of an experiment, and its possible values correspond to the real numbers in the interval $(0, \\infty).$\n\n- Statement III describes a continuous random variable. Its value is determined by the result of an experiment, and its possible values correspond to the real numbers in the interval $(0, 1\\,000].$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105368 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "16a6f0f7-4003-5908-8b1b-68d218fdcb8f" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether a Function Is a Valid PDF"}] :question/id #uuid "295d2025-c958-55e3-a194-5cdb271a510c" :question/math-academy-id "e-10838" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040017/assets/26d09b87ebbabe4c1e1dcd8d5442b1d3efce216d7f404808a3777b8ffa3304ff.png)\n\nConsider the function $f(x)$ whose graph is shown above.\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{8}({x}^{2}+1),\\, & -1<x\\le 1, \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- $f(x) \\geq 0$ for all $x$\n\n- $\\displaystyle \\int_{-1}^1 f(x) \\,\\textrm{d}x = 1$\n\n- $f(x)$ is a valid probability density function" :question/worked-solution "For a function $f(x)$ to be a valid probability density function on a set $S,$ it must satisfy the following conditions:\n\n- $f(x) \\geq 0$ for all $x$\n\n- $\\displaystyle \\int_{S} f(x) \\, \\textrm dx = 1$\n\nNow, let's consider each statement in turn:\n\n- Statement I is true. From the graph, we see that $f(x)$ is nonnegative for all $x.$\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}\\frac{3}{8}({x}^{2}+1)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{8}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{x}^{2}+1\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{8}{[\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3}+x]}_{-1}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{8}([\\frac{{1}^{3}}{3}+1]-[\\frac{(-1)^{3}}{3}+(-1)]) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{8}([\\frac{1}{3}+1]-[-\\frac{1}{3}-1]) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{8}(\\frac{4}{3}-(-\\frac{4}{3})) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{8}\\cdot \\frac{8}{3} \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since statements I and II are true, $f(x)$ is a valid probability density function.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105377 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f59bd7df-afa9-5dcf-9dab-fc7f3cf291fa" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Value of an Unknown Constant Given a PDF"}] :question/id #uuid "32131e34-3141-51b3-8d26-6cee481f168c" :question/math-academy-id "e-11037" :question/problem "Solve for $k$ given that the following function is a probability density function:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}2{x}^{3}+k{x}^{2},\\, & 0<x<1, \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise.}\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "For a function $f(x)$ to be a valid probability density function on a set $S,$ it must satisfy the following conditions:\n\n- $f(x) \\geq 0$ for all $x\\in S$\n\n- $\\displaystyle \\int_{S} f(x) \\, \\textrm dx = 1$\n\nFor the given function, the second condition states that\n\n$$\n\\int_0^{1} (2x^3+kx^2) \\, \\textrm dx = 1.\n$$\n\nComputing the integral on the left-hand side, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(2{x}^{3}+k{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x & ={[\\frac{{x}^{4}}{2}+\\frac{k{x}^{3}}{3}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =[\\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{k}{3}]-0 \\\\  & =\\frac{k}{3}+\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\dfrac k3+\\dfrac{1}{2} = 1 \\quad \\Longrightarrow \\quad k=\\dfrac{3}{2}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186305032 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0ee39554-fd77-5e90-baa8-f9320ffc3398" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Continuous Random Variables Given in Context"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "172da751-b7b1-5993-92ce-0b3a89fc22b6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305034} {:answer/id #uuid "cba6ca18-28a9-5486-94cd-df83dc6c91fa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305035} {:answer/id #uuid "c5612fe7-40d4-514a-8685-6d64f1a2fd2f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305036} {:answer/id #uuid "790cf3d3-633d-5625-b997-6c1db9d21bc8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305037} {:answer/id #uuid "fe2bbd98-032e-589e-acc1-f778f65eceac" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305038}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "790cf3d3-633d-5625-b997-6c1db9d21bc8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305037} :answer-field/id #uuid "cc281f46-91de-5d1b-9764-36cdc503c98f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305033}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "78edb6bd-ed48-54d3-90d2-5d11cd8b4ff4" :question/math-academy-id "q-126053" :question/problem "Which of the following are continuous random variables?\n\n- The volume, measured in cubic centimeters, of water in a cup of coffee\n- The average length, measured in millimeters, of hairs on a horse\n- The number of hairs on a cat" :question/worked-solution "A random variable is a variable whose value is determined by the outcome of a trial or experiment. A *continuous* random variable is a random variable whose possible values are represented by an interval of real numbers.\n\nCommon examples of continuous random variables are shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040017/assets/05e3d893f6c069dce5b51dc7d9fa5830b44bca58fa071009548fde20ebe92c19.png)\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each of the given statements.\n\n- Statement I describes a continuous random variable. Its value is determined by the result of an experiment, and its possible values correspond to the real interval $(0,\\infty ).$\n\n- Statement II describes a continuous random variable. Its value is determined by the result of an experiment, and its possible values correspond to the real interval $(0,\\infty ).$\n\n- Statement III does *not* describe a continuous random variable. Its value is determined by the result of an experiment, but its possible values are the integers $0,1,2,3,…,$ which do not correspond to an interval of real numbers.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186305039 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "16a6f0f7-4003-5908-8b1b-68d218fdcb8f" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether a Function Is a Valid PDF"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "09fc0563-1988-57f7-bb4c-7d963fcceb97" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305041} {:answer/id #uuid "a089c314-9a63-5f70-9807-f86b76748344" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305042} {:answer/id #uuid "57a88cc7-240e-5e66-af6b-5db9a3880227" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305043} {:answer/id #uuid "771dcc0d-7986-5c88-8d66-a61d9bd57629" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305044} {:answer/id #uuid "a7ae8ded-5727-59c9-9a75-04864edb3a09" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305045}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "09fc0563-1988-57f7-bb4c-7d963fcceb97" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305041} :answer-field/id #uuid "a5416699-4477-5a8c-886f-c7fd7a060bd5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305040}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "5c694066-1106-59d2-ad9b-3f01367d7aea" :question/math-academy-id "q-158419" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040017/assets/4acac98ef7276cb1e90557ca161666a33ccf0a7627b9ebfcbbe44508b9e5f5bb.png)\n\nConsider the function $f(x)$ whose graph is shown above.\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}x+\\frac{3}{2}{x}^{2},\\, & 0<x\\le 1, \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- $f(x)\\ge 0$ for all $x$\n- ${\\int }_{0}^{1}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=1$\n- $f(x)$ is a valid probability density function" :question/worked-solution "For a function $f(x)$ to be a valid probability density function on a set $S,$ it must satisfy the following conditions:\n\n- $f(x)\\ge 0$ for all $x\\in S$\n\n- ${\\int }_{S}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=1$\n\nNow, let's consider each statement in turn:\n\n- Statement I is true. From the graph, we see that $f(x)$ is nonnegative for all $x.$\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(x+\\frac{3}{2}{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}+\\frac{{x}^{3}}{2}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =[\\frac{{1}^{2}}{2}+\\frac{{1}^{3}}{2}]-[\\frac{{0}^{2}}{2}+\\frac{{0}^{3}}{2}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{1}{2}-0 \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since statements I and II are true, $f(x)$ is a valid probability density function.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186305053 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "16a6f0f7-4003-5908-8b1b-68d218fdcb8f" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether a Function Is a Valid PDF"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "51366a49-fd12-5786-8f7d-f07bc638c401" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305055} {:answer/id #uuid "997c191b-7355-5162-8345-97f79fb7d478" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305056} {:answer/id #uuid "437df922-4149-5cc6-94d7-2f7829f9639e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305057} {:answer/id #uuid "40f5ebcd-03b2-513b-839f-8c5b12075845" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305058} {:answer/id #uuid "5718d1cc-559e-5b30-ac50-6efe49b4568a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305059}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "40f5ebcd-03b2-513b-839f-8c5b12075845" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305058} :answer-field/id #uuid "84690231-57c0-5dab-af90-fa45238c5de9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305054}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "3503a51d-9484-5e3d-a810-963c28370c44" :question/math-academy-id "q-158427" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040017/assets/ae6d25fae4127a74b38bcb9c0de8f44705dca81353c89557047e5d35a3465c5b.png)\n\nConsider the function $f(x)$ whose graph is shown above.\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}2x,\\, & 1\\le x\\le 2, \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- $f(x)\\ge 0$ for all $x$\n- ${\\int }_{1}^{2}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=1$\n- $f(x)$ is a valid probability density function" :question/worked-solution "For a function $f(x)$ to be a valid probability density function on a set $S,$ it must satisfy the following conditions:\n\n- $f(x)\\ge 0$ for all $x$\n\n- ${\\int }_{S}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=1$\n\nNow, let's consider each statement in turn:\n\n- Statement I is true. From the graph, we see that $f(x)$ is nonnegative for all $x.$\n\n- Statement II is false. Indeed,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{2}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}2x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[{x}^{2}]}_{1}^{2} \\\\  & =4-1 \\\\  & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since statement II is false, $f(x)$ is not a valid probability density function.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186305088 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f59bd7df-afa9-5dcf-9dab-fc7f3cf291fa" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Value of an Unknown Constant Given a PDF"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3b2eaee4-b5bc-5e8b-810c-e8d157ffd3bc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305090} {:answer/id #uuid "3a3b9c66-3a41-5161-80eb-07798c4cd034" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305091} {:answer/id #uuid "2e0eb847-8d62-591e-8b45-6ab03a14bc41" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305092} {:answer/id #uuid "6c0a7ba7-ed75-5374-aa83-b7ecf6165e16" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305093} {:answer/id #uuid "9d062a76-0166-5376-b966-5c2b21f76c77" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "24" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305094}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9d062a76-0166-5376-b966-5c2b21f76c77" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "24" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305094} :answer-field/id #uuid "7a332853-7bb0-59d4-a686-56e556a86931" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305089}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "87d23167-a7f7-5483-8432-8ac06af4b428" :question/math-academy-id "q-158447" :question/problem "Solve for $k$ given that the following function is a probability density function:\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}+1}{k},\\, & 1<x<4, \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise.}\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "For a function $f(x)$ to be a valid probability density function on a set $S,$ it must satisfy the following conditions:\n\n- $f(x)\\ge 0$ for all $x\\in S$\n\n- ${\\int }_{S}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=1$\n\nFor the given function, the second condition states that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{1}^{4}\\frac{{x}^{2}+1}{k}\\,\\text{d}x=1.\n$$\n\nComputing the integral on the left-hand side, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{4}\\frac{{x}^{2}+1}{k}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{k}{\\int }_{1}^{4}({x}^{2}+1)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{k}{[\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3}+x]}_{1}^{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{k}([\\frac{64}{3}+4]-[\\frac{1}{3}+1]) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{k}(21+3) \\\\  & =\\frac{24}{k}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{24}{k}=1\\,⟹\\,k=24.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106473 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f59bd7df-afa9-5dcf-9dab-fc7f3cf291fa" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Value of an Unknown Constant Given a PDF"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "91de6dfc-3550-5227-826f-617909d90dc6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186105348} {:answer/id #uuid "becf5a88-f447-5bbc-9a00-579a74ecfe2c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186105349} {:answer/id #uuid "e6038154-3664-5600-a7d9-77e6082263e1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186105350} {:answer/id #uuid "0d5fddf9-5def-5d25-b2b2-98d44685cfd1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{9}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186105351} {:answer/id #uuid "450ea2f9-252f-5675-b796-03caa076d7ff" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186105352}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0d5fddf9-5def-5d25-b2b2-98d44685cfd1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{9}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186105351} :answer-field/id #uuid "6b3d8b68-2035-520d-82f9-f67ea75460d1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186105347}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "57ead9aa-937c-57a1-8fa5-6d07fce39ffb" :question/math-academy-id "q-98912" :question/problem "Solve for $k$ given that the following function is a probability density function:\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}\\frac{k}{{x}^{2}},\\, & 3\\le x\\le 9, \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise.}\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "For a function $f(x)$ to be a valid probability density function on a set $S,$ it must satisfy the following conditions:\n\n- $f(x)\\ge 0$ for all $x\\in S$\n\n- ${\\int }_{S}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=1$\n\nFor the given function, the second condition states that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{3}^{9}\\frac{k}{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x=1.\n$$\n\nComputing the integral on the left-hand side, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{3}^{9}\\frac{k}{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{3}^{9}k{x}^{-2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[-\\frac{k}{x}]}_{3}^{9} \\\\  & =-\\frac{k}{9}+\\frac{k}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{2k}{9}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{2k}{9}=1\\,⟹\\,k=\\frac{9}{2}.\n$$"}]}
